\newcommand{\myabstract} % to avoid duplicating the abstract for the TR and journal article version.
{
%	Crowdsourced delivery utilizes the services of independent individuals whose acceptance behavior are uncertain and cannot be fully controlled by an operator, as opposed to traditional delivery modes. We consider a setting in which an operator groups tasks into bundles and offers the resulting bundles to occasional drivers in exchange for some compensation to minimize the total expected cost of deliveries. Uncertainty in the occasional drivers' acceptance behavior of the bundles is considered via (individual) acceptance probability functions. We focus on logistic acceptance probability functions whose main parameters depend on the bundle and driver attributes, and the compensation. We propose a mixed-integer nonlinear program with an exponential number of variables that simultaneously decides (i) how to group tasks into bundles, (ii) which bundles are offered to which occasional drivers, and (iii) the compensation offered to occasional drivers for the bundles. We present exact column generation algorithms where the pricing subproblem corresponds to an elementary shortest path problem with resource constraints (ESPPRC) and a non-linear and non-additive objective function. We develop a tailored algorithm for this variant of the ESPPRC that exploits the theoretical bounds on the duration of the routes of the occasional drivers and the dominance rules capturing the properties of the objective function. The computational experiments show that the proposed algorithm is capable of solving large instances in a reasonable amount of time. \red{ \#\#\#\#\#\# Internal Comment: Highlight the benefits of embracing our model over the existing ones.}

% Context
Challenges in last-mile delivery such as high costs, rising customer expectations, and congested urban traffic have encouraged innovative solutions like crowdsourced delivery, where online platforms leverage the services of drivers who occasionally perform delivery tasks for compensation. 
%Although initial modeling efforts in crowdsourced delivery focused on single-task assignments, the emphasis is now shifting toward bundling multiple tasks. 
% Challenge
A key challenge 
%in crowdsourced delivery 
is that occasional drivers’ acceptance behavior towards offered tasks is uncertain and influenced by task properties and compensation.
%properties of the offers created by the platform, such as the bundle of tasks and compensation amount.
The current literature lacks formulations that fully address this challenge. 
% The problem
Hence, we formulate an integrated optimization problem that maximizes total expected cost savings %for the platform
by offering bundles of tasks to occasional drivers. To this end, we simultaneously determine the optimal set of bundles % task bundles,
%s to bundle, 
their assignment to occasional drivers, and compensations for each bundle-driver pair while considering bundle- and compensation-dependent acceptance probabilities, %of occasional drivers
which are captured via generic logistic functions. % with arbitrary predictors.
% The solution
The vast number of potential bundles, combined with %the nonlinear objective function—resulting from 
incorporating %occasional drivers' 
acceptance probabilities 
%\blue{,via generic logistic functions with arbitrary predictors,} %of bundle and compensation decisions—
leads to a mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) formulation with exponentially many variables. 
%\blue{The MINLP uses generic logistic functions with arbitrary predictors to model occasional drivers’ acceptance probabilities.}
Using mild assumptions, we address these complexities by exploiting properties of the problem, leading to an exact linearization of the MINLP which we solve via a tailored exact column generation algorithm. %The 
Our algorithm uses a variant of the elementary shortest path problem with resource constraints (ESPPRC) that features a non-linear and non-additive objective function as its subproblem, for which we develop tailored dominance and pruning strategies. 
% Comparison with the existing approaches
%Our approach is superior to the current state-of-the-art methods from the modeling perspective, since it optimizes bundling, compensation, and assignment decisions simultaneously and exactly, accounting for every possible bundle configuration and acceptance probabilities of occasional drivers.
% Experiments
We introduce several heuristic and exact algorithm variants, and perform
%run 
an extensive set of computational experiments evaluating the performance of algorithms and the structure of the solutions.
% Technical results 
The results demonstrate the efficiency of exact and heuristic algorithm variants for instances with up to 120 tasks and 60 drivers, and highlight the advantages of integrated decision-making over sequential approaches in line with the recent literature.
% Managerial insight
The sensitivity analysis indicates that sensitivity to compensation is the most influential factor in shaping the bundle structure.
}

