\title{Integration of a categorical operad}
\author{Dominik Trnka}
\address{Department of Mathematics and Statistics, Masaryk University, Kotl\'a\v rsk\'a 2, 61137 Brno; Institute of Mathematics, Czech Academy of Sciences, {\v Z}itn{\'a} 25, 115 67 Prague 1; and Institute of Mathematics, University of Technology, Anton\'insk\'a 548/1, 602 00 Brno.}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
I describe Grothendieck construction for non-symmetric operads with values in  categories, and hence in groupoids and posets. The construction produces a 2-category which is operadicaly fibered over the category $\Delta_s$ of finite non-empty ordinals and surjections. I~describe an inverse for the construction, yielding a 1-1 correspondence of non-symmetric categorical operads and operadic 2-categories (split-)fibered over $\Delta_s$, which resembles the correspondence of categorical presheaves and fibered categories. The result provides a new characterization of non-symmetric categorical operads and tools to study them.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle
To integrate means to make something \textit{whole}. 
An operad $\tP$ is a compositional structure consisting of separate pieces $\tP_n$ of abstract $n$-ary operations, and rules how to compose them.
By integrating an operad we mean finding a new bigger object which contains all the operations of the operad and faithfully encodes the composition of operations. 
We also require, that the operad can be reconstructed from its integration. 
For $\Set$-valued operads, an integration is given by the \textit{operadic Grothendieck construction} of \cite{Batanin_Markl:duoidal}, resulting in the \textit{operadic category} $\int\tP$. The article \cite{Batanin_Markl:duoidal} introduces the theory of operadic categories, which is a unifying framework for general operadic structures and their comparison. The integration is one of the features of the theory and it is available for a general $\bO$-operad together with a correspondence
$$\oper{\bO}{\Set} \simeq \textrm{DoFib}(\bO)$$ 
 between $\Set$-valued $\bO$-operads and discrete operadic fibrations over an operadic category~$\bO$. The $\bO$-operads include classical symmetric and non-symmetric operads, colored operads, or graph-based operads (hyperoperads) governing cyclic or modular
operads, wheeled properads, dioperads, $\frac{1}{2}$PROPs, permutads, or pre-permutads.

To draw a connection, recall the classical Grothendieck construction (integration) of a categorical presheaf. For a functor $F\colon \C^{\mathrm{op}} \to \Cat$, the category $\int_\C F$ has objects $[C,a]$, where~$C\in\C$ and $a\in FC.$ The morphisms 
$[C,a]\to[D,b]$ are pairs $[f;\alpha]$ with $f\colon C \to D$ in $\C$ and $\alpha\colon Ff(b)\to a$ in $FC$.
The term \textit{integration} is already suggested by the commonly used symbol~$\int$ for the construction, however it is not a common terminology. There is a fully faithful functor
$$\int_\C\colon \mathrm{Fun}(\C^{\mathrm{op}},Cat) \to \Cat/\C,$$
which establishes a 2-equivalence of categorical presheaves and categorical fibrations over $\C$. For a detailed treatment cf.~eg.~\cite[Ch.~9 \& 10]{JY}.

The current text is a follow-up to \cite{T2}, which develops the integration for categorical operads of \textit{unary} operadic categories. 
I derive the results for constant-free non-symmetric categorical operads, i.e. operads over the operadic category $\Delta_s$ of finite non-empty ordinals and surjections. 
The operadic category $\Delta_s$ in not unary, but is still relatively simple. This~text makes a step towards the integration of categorical $\bO$-operads of a general operadic category~$\bO$.

The operadic integration can be used to combining two compatible structures on one set. In~Example~\ref{example:trees2} we deal with planar rooted trees together with the operation of grafting (operadic structure) and edge contraction (poset structure). As a result, the integration of the categorical operad of trees $\int\sT$ is a 2-category whose morphisms combine the two structures in a natural way. There is a strict factorization system on $\int\sT$ which factors a general morphisms as \textit{contractions} followed by \textit{cuts}, cf.~Proposition~\ref{proposition:factorisation} and the diagrams below it.

I believe that the problem of integration could be alternatively approached, and~possibly was in some form, by the frameworks of $T$-multicategories \cite{leinster}, polynomial monads \cite{Batanin_Berger:polynomial}, or Feynman categories \cite{Kaufmann_Ward:Fey}. The results of this text are summarized below.

\noindent\textsc{Results:}
For a constant-free non-symmetric operad $\tP$, a 2-category $\int\tP$ is constructed, together with a projection $\pi\colon\tP\to\Delta_s$. 
I describe its properties and introduce a 2-categorical generalization of operadic categories in Definition~\ref{definition:operadic_2_cat}. I also introduce non-discrete version of operadic fibrations and show that the projection $\pi$ is a splitting operadic fibration, cf.~Definitions~\ref{definition:fibration}, \ref{definition:splitting} and Theorem~\ref{theorem:intP_is_split-fibered}.
I arrive at Theorem~\ref{theorem:correspondence} which gives the 1-1 correspondence of categorical non-symmetric operads and split-fibered operadic 2-categories. Lastly, Proposition~\ref{proposition:classical_fibrations} relates the results to the standard case of categorical presheaves and categorical fibrations.

To close this introductory section, I comment on categorical operads and where to find them. 
In~the literature, categorical operads often appear either as operads with values in groupoids, or operads valued in partially ordered sets. Some of the most classical operads also carry a natural structure of a poset or groupoid. 
Namely, there is the Tamari order on the set of planar binary rooted trees. A remarkable application of operads valued in groupoids is \cite{Fresse} on Grothendieck--Teichmüller groups. 
Operads valued in posets appear e.g.~in \cite{Berger97} and many poset-valued operads are described in \cite{Bashkirov}. Categorical operads are further considered, for instance, in \cite{Batanin08,CG14,elmen}.
A concrete example of a (non-strict) categorical operad is the operad of leveled trees. The operations in arity $n$ are leveled planar rooted trees with $n$ leaves, and there is a unique isomorphism of trees if they differ by an admissible change of leveling, cf.~Figure~\ref{fig:leveled_trees}. This operad plays a key r{\^o}le in the construction of free operads~\cite{Batanin_Markl:kodu2021,moje}. 

