Nekbone uses the PCG method (Figure \ref{PCG_framework_fig}) in double precision to solve the Poisson/Helmholtz equation for scalar/vector fields within a box-shaped domain. The domain is divided into $E = n_x \times n_y \times n_z$ equally sized box elements. As a proxy application, Nekbone employs either simple preconditioning (JACOBI) or no preconditioning (COPY). This setup restricts computation to the axhelm kernel (Table \ref{axhelm_time_Nekbone_others_work}) and communication to the gather-scatter kernel \cite{OpenACC_acceleration_Nekbone, Parallel_Communication_Nekbone, Nekbone_GPUs_OpenACC_CUDA, HipBone_2023}, making Nekbone a minimal yet representative framework for studying the two most critical matrix-free kernels in HOSFEM.
