\subsection{Axhelm: Core Computation in HOSFEM}
\label{axhelm_subsection}
\begin{definition}[Tensor Contraction]
  \label{sum_factorization_DEF}
  Let $\mathbf{x}$ be an $N_1^3$ vector. Let $\mathbf{y}_r = \mathbf{D}_r \mathbf{x}$, $\mathbf{y}_s = \mathbf{D}_s \mathbf{x}$, and $\mathbf{y}_t = \mathbf{D}_t \mathbf{x}$ ($\mathbf{D}_r^\mathrm{T}$, $\mathbf{D}_s^\mathrm{T}$, and $\mathbf{D}_t^\mathrm{T}$ are similar). Then, $\mathbf{y}_r$, $\mathbf{y}_s$, and $\mathbf{y}_t$ can be computed as follows for $0 \leq i, j, k \leq N$:
  \begin{equation}
    \begin{matrix}
      \label{sum_factorization_eq}
      \mathbf{y}_r(i, j, k) = \sum_{0 \leq m \leq N} \hat{\mathbf{D}}_N(i, m) \mathbf{x}(m, j, k), \\
      \mathbf{y}_s(i, j, k) = \sum_{0 \leq m \leq N} \hat{\mathbf{D}}_N(j, m) \mathbf{x}(i, m, k), \\
      \mathbf{y}_t(i, j, k) = \sum_{0 \leq m \leq N} \hat{\mathbf{D}}_N(k, m) \mathbf{x}(i, j, m),
    \end{matrix}
  \end{equation}
  where the 3D index $(i,j,k)$ represents the $i + j N_1 + k N_1^2$-th row.
\end{definition}

The kernel used to compute $\mathbf{Y}^{(e)} = \mathbf{A}^{(e)} \mathbf{X}^{(e)}$ has various names, such as Ax \cite{wind_energy_simulations, HP_SEM_FPGA}, AX \cite{Accelerate_Nekbone_on_FPGA},or ax3D \cite{OpenACC_acceleration_Nekbone}. This paper adopts the name axhelm \cite{Accelerate_axhelm_in_Nekbone_on_A64FX, nekBench_website}. As an example, consider the case when $d = 1$ (scalar field: $\mathbf{X}^{(e)}$ has only one column). One of the state-of-the-art implementations of axhelm from Nekbone/RS is presented in Algorithm \ref{algorithm_axhelm_original}. It employs the 2D thread block structure \cite{Acceleration_tensor_product_operations_in_FEM}: 1) Each block processes for a single element $e$. 2) Each block employs a layer of $N_1 \times N_1$ threads to process $N_1$ k-layers in lock-step.

The primary computational workload (Lines 15-16, 24-25) arises from six tensor contractions (Definition \ref{sum_factorization_DEF}), each involving the multiplication of a tensor-product (e.g., $\mathbf{D}_r$) with a vector, requiring $2N_1^4$ FLOPs. Tensor contraction arises from the sum factorization technique \cite{High_Order_Methods_Incompressible_Fluid_Flow, NekRS_first_paper, wind_energy_simulations}, which exploits the local tensor-product structure to reduce the computational complexity of $\mathbf{Y}^{(e)} = \mathbf{A}^{(e)} \mathbf{X}^{(e)}$ from $\mathcal{O}(N_1^6)$ to $\mathcal{O}(N_1^4)$. This is the fundamental source of HOSFEM's high performance. In addition, Lines 17-21 incorporate the geometric and scalar factors. For Poisson equation ($\lambda_0 = 1$ and $\lambda_1 = 0$), Line 10, 21 and "$\lambda_0 *$" in Lines 17-19 are unnecessary.

\begin{algorithm}
  \fontsize{7.8}{7.8}\selectfont
  \caption{Original axhelm kernel for a scalar field}
  \label{algorithm_axhelm_original}
  \SetKwInOut{Input}{\textbf{Input}}
  \SetKwInOut{Output}{\textbf{Output}}
  \SetKwInOut{Init}{\textbf{Init}}
  \SetKw{KwDStep}{\textbf{step}}
  \SetKw{KwDAnd}{\textbf{and}}
  \SetKw{KwDIn}{\textbf{in}}
  \Input{$\mathbf{\Lambda}_0^{(e)}, \mathbf{\Lambda}_1^{(e)}, \mathbf{G}_{00}^{(e)}, ..., \mathbf{G}_{22}^{(e)}, \mathbf{G}_\mathrm{wj}^{(e)}, \mathbf{X}^{(e)}, \hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$.
  }
  \Output{$\mathbf{Y}^{(e)}$ (both input and output are in global memory).
  }
  e = blockIdx.x; j = threadIdx.y; i = threadIdx.x\;
  s\_Dhat[j][i] = $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$(j,i)\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}'s\_' means shared memory}
  \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{k} \leq N$}{
    x[k] = $\mathbf{X}^{(e)}$(i,j,k); y[k] = 0\;
  }
  syncthreads()\;
  \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{k} \leq N$}{
    g00 = $\mathbf{G}_{00}^{(e)}$(i,j,k); g01 = $\mathbf{G}_{01}^{(e)}$(i,j,k); g02 = $\mathbf{G}_{02}^{(e)}$(i,j,k)\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}only}
    g11 = $\mathbf{G}_{11}^{(e)}$(i,j,k); g12 = $\mathbf{G}_{12}^{(e)}$(i,j,k); g22 = $\mathbf{G}_{22}^{(e)}$(i,j,k)\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}access}
    \If{$\mathrm{isHelm}$}{
      gwj = $\mathbf{G}_\mathrm{wj}^{(e)}$(i,j,k); $\lambda_0$ = $\mathbf{\Lambda}_0^{(e)}$(i,j,k); $\lambda_1$ = $\mathbf{\Lambda}_1^{(e)}$(i,j,k)\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}diag.}
    }
    s\_x[j][i] = x[k]\;
    syncthreads()\;
    xr = 0; xs = 0; xt = 0; tmp = 0\;
    \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{m} \leq N$}{
      xr+=s\_Dhat[i][m]*s\_x[j][m]\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}$\mathbf{D}_r, \mathbf{D}_s, \mathbf{D}_t$}
      xs+=s\_Dhat[j][m]*s\_x[m][i];\quad xt+=s\_Dhat[k][m]*x[m]\;
    }
    s\_gxr[j][i] = $\lambda_0$ * (g00 * xr + g01 * xs + g02 * xt)\;
    s\_gxs[j][i] = $\lambda_0$ * (g01 * xr + g11 * xs + g12 * xt)\;
    gxt\hspace{0.825cm} = $\lambda_0$ * (g02 * xr + g12 * xs + g22 * xt)\;
    \If{$\mathrm{isHelm}$}{
      tmp = gwj * $\lambda_1$ * x[k]\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}isHelm: Helmholtz/Poisson}
    }
    syncthreads()\;
    \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{m} \leq N$}{
      tmp+=s\_Dhat[m][i]*s\_gxr[j][m]\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}$\mathbf{D}_r^\mathrm{T}, \mathbf{D}_s^\mathrm{T}, \mathbf{D}_t^\mathrm{T}$}
      tmp+=s\_Dhat[m][j]*s\_gxs[m][i];\ \ y[m]+=s\_Dhat[k][m]*gxt\;
    }
    y[k] += tmp\;
  }
  \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{k} \leq N$}{
    $\mathbf{Y}^{(e)}$(i,j,k) = y[k]\;
  }
\end{algorithm}











\section{The Proposed Method}
\label{The_Proposed_Method_section}
\subsection{Bottleneck Analysis of Axhelm}
\label{Performance_bottleneck_analysis_of_axhelm_subsection}
Different studies suggest varying appropriate ranges for $N$: $7 \leq N \leq 11$ \cite{High_Fidelity_Flow_Solver_on_FPGA}, $N \leq 10$ \cite{NekRS_website}, and $7 \leq N \leq 15$ (Table \ref{axhelm_time_Nekbone_others_work}). Although some studies investigate smaller values \cite{Scalability_of_high_performance_PDE_solvers}, the most commonly used $N$ is 7 \cite{NekRS_first_paper, SC23_HOSFEM, Reducing_communication_in_CG_HOSFEM, Neko_first_paper, Nek5000_RS_Performance_on_Advanced_GPU, High_Fidelity_Flow_Solver_on_FPGA, Accelerate_axhelm_in_Nekbone_on_A64FX}. For a single element, the computation of the axhelm requires: 1) $12N_1^4$ FLOPs for six tensor contractions. 2) $15N_1^3$ FLOPs for geometric factors. 3) Additional $5N_1^3$ FLOPs for the scale factors in the Helmholtz equation. The above computational cost needs to be multiplied by $d$. Apart from the differentiation matrix, the total global memory access consists of two main parts:
\begin{align}
  \label{geometric_factor_memory_access}
  \text{geometric factors}:&\ \ \mathcal{M}_\text{geo} = (6 + \text{isHelm})N_1^3 \times \text{FPSize},           \\
  \label{other_memory_access}
  \mathbf{X}^{(e)}\mathbf{Y}^{(e)}\mathbf{\Lambda}_0^{(e)}\mathbf{\Lambda}_1^{(e)}:&\ \ \mathcal{M}_\text{XYL} = (2 \times \text{isHelm} + 2d)N_1^3 \times \text{FPSize}.
\end{align}
Here, \text{isHelm} is a binary value (0 or 1), indicating whether the Helmholtz equation is being solved, and \text{FPSize} refers to the size of the floating-point data type. The above statistics are summarized in Table \ref{table_workload_memory_access}. The operational intensity is defined as $\mathcal{I} = \mathcal{F}/\mathcal{M}$.
\begin{table}[!htb]
  \caption{Computational workload and memory access requirements for the original axhelm kernels.}
  \label{table_workload_memory_access}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
  \hline
  \textbf{Kernel Type} & $\mathcal{F}_\mathrm{ax}$ (\textbf{FLOP}) & $\mathcal{M}_\mathrm{orig}$ (\textbf{Byte}) \\
  \hline
  Poisson, $d = 1$ & $12N_1^4 + 15N_1^3$ & $(8N_1^3 + N_1^2) \times \text{FPSize}$ \\
  Helmholtz, $d = 1$ & $12N_1^4 + 20N_1^3$ & $(11N_1^3 + N_1^2) \times \text{FPSize}$ \\
  Poisson, $d = 3$ & $36N_1^4 + 45N_1^3$ & $(12N_1^3 + N_1^2) \times \text{FPSize}$ \\
  Helmholtz, $d = 3$ & $36N_1^4 + 60N_1^3$ & $(15N_1^3 + N_1^2) \times \text{FPSize}$ \\
  \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The platform's peak-to-bandwidth ratio is defined as $\text{PBR} = \frac{\mathcal{P}_\text{peak}}{\mathcal{B}}$, where $\mathcal{P}_\text{peak}$ is the theoretical peak performance and $\mathcal{B}$ is the maximum achievable global memory bandwidth. PBR is the minimum operational intensity required to achieve $\mathcal{P}_\text{peak}$ \cite{Roofline_model_first_paper}.

Figure \ref{operational_intensity_fig} shows that at $N_1 = 18$, the operational intensity of the axhelm kernel (Poisson, $d = 3$) exceeds one of the PBR lines. The following observations can be made: 1) The operational intensity shows a linear growth trend as $N$ increases. 2) The axhelm's performance is constrained by memory bandwidth in real-world applications.
Therefore, previous studies on the axhelm performance optimization have typically utilized a performance model \cite{Acceleration_tensor_product_operations_in_FEM, Optimization_Ax_Nekbone_SC_20_Poster} based on memory access bandwidth limitations:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{R}_\text{orig} = \mathcal{B} \cdot \frac{\mathcal{F}_\text{ax}}{\mathcal{M}_\text{orig}}.
\end{equation}
Under this model, related work has seemingly approached their respective theoretical performance limits. This implies that continuing to optimize tensor contractions is no longer effective. To achieve further optimization, memory access must be reduced. Meanwhile, we note that the geometric factors account for a significant portion of memory access, but contribute only lower-order terms ($\mathcal{O}(N_1^3)$) to the computational workload. So far, this paper, like most studies of the axhelm, treats the geometric factors as abstract constants. Next, we will clearly outline their computation process.
