\subsection{Computation of Geometric Factors}
\label{Computation_of_Geometric_Factors_subsection}
The geometric factors are related to the Jacobian matrix. For each element $e$, the mapping $\Phi^{(e)}: \hat{\Omega} \to \Omega^{(e)}$, $(r, s, t) \mapsto (x, y, z)$,
uniquely determines the Jacobian matrix function:
\begin{equation}
  \label{Jacobian_matrix_function}
  \mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}}(r, s, t) :=
  \begin{bmatrix}
    \frac{\partial {\Phi^{(e)}}(r, s, t)}{\partial r} \\
    \frac{\partial {\Phi^{(e)}}(r, s, t)}{\partial s} \\
    \frac{\partial {\Phi^{(e)}}(r, s, t)}{\partial t}
  \end{bmatrix}^\mathrm{T} =
  \begin{bmatrix}
    \frac{\partial x}{\partial r} & \frac{\partial x}{\partial s} & \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} \\
    \frac{\partial y}{\partial r} & \frac{\partial y}{\partial s} & \frac{\partial y}{\partial t} \\
    \frac{\partial z}{\partial r} & \frac{\partial z}{\partial s} & \frac{\partial z}{\partial t}
  \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
This defines a $3 \times 3$ matrix at any point $(r, s, t) \in \hat{\Omega} = [-1, 1]^3$. Therefore, at each physical node $(e, i, j, k)$, there is a corresponding, unique Jacobian matrix of size $3 \times 3$:
\begin{equation}
  \label{Jacobian_matrix_at_node}
  \mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)} := \mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}}(\xi_i, \xi_j, \xi_k).
\end{equation}
The 7 geometric factors at the physical node are then defined as:
\begin{equation}
  \label{Jacobian_matrix_to_geometric_factors}
  w_i w_j w_k |\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}| \cdot {\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}}^{-1} {\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}}^\mathrm{-T} \quad\text{and}\quad w_i w_j w_k |\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}|,
\end{equation}
where the latter is a scalar, and the former is a $3 \times 3$ symmetric matrix (with only 6 values needed). The computational cost of obtaining the geometric factors from the Jacobian matrix is easily estimated, primarily involving the calculation of a $3 \times 3$ determinant and the inverse of a $3 \times 3$ matrix. 

The problem "How to cost-effectively obtain the geometric factors" can be reformulated as "How to cost-effectively obtain the Jacobian matrix." However, for a general $\Phi^{(e)}$ without an analytical expression, we can only obtain the Jacobian matrix through the following discrete and transposed form of Equation \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_function}:
{\small
\begin{equation}
  \label{Jacobian_matrix_tensor_contraction}
  \begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{D} \mathbf{C}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} & =
    \begin{bmatrix}
      \mathbf{D}_r \mathbf{C}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} \\
      \mathbf{D}_s \mathbf{C}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} \\
      \mathbf{D}_t \mathbf{C}_\text{nodes}^{(e)}
    \end{bmatrix} =
    \begin{bmatrix}
      \mathbf{D}_r \mathbf{x}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} & \mathbf{D}_r \mathbf{y}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} & \mathbf{D}_r \mathbf{z}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} \\
      \mathbf{D}_s \mathbf{x}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} & \mathbf{D}_s \mathbf{y}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} & \mathbf{D}_s \mathbf{z}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} \\
      \mathbf{D}_t \mathbf{x}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} & \mathbf{D}_t \mathbf{y}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} & \mathbf{D}_t \mathbf{z}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} \\
    \end{bmatrix}
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
}
Here, $\mathbf{C}_\text{nodes}^{(e)} = [\mathbf{x}_\text{nodes}^{(e)}, \mathbf{y}_\text{nodes}^{(e)}, \mathbf{z}_\text{nodes}^{(e)}]$ is the $N_1^3 \times 3$ matrix of physical node coordinates in element $e$, which is naturally the discrete form of $\Phi^{(e)}$ as a sampled subset. $\mathbf{D}_r$, $\mathbf{D}_s$, and $\mathbf{D}_t$ represent the discrete forms of the partial differential operators $\frac{\partial}{\partial r}$, $\frac{\partial}{\partial s}$, and $\frac{\partial}{\partial t}$, respectively. The final result is a $3N_1^3 \times 3$ matrix representing the $3 \times 3$ Jacobian matrix of the $N_1^3$ nodes in element $e$.
\begin{equation*}
  \begin{aligned}
     & \text{Continuous:} & \Phi^{(e)}\quad                    & \xrightarrow{\text{Eq. } \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_function}}           & \mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}} &                                                                                                 \\
     &                    &                                    &                                                                      & \downarrow\quad         & \text{Eq. } \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_at_node}                                                     \\
     & \text{Discrete:}   & \mathbf{C}_\text{nodes}^{(e)}\quad & \xrightarrow{\text{Eq. } \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_tensor_contraction}} & \mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}  & \xrightarrow{\text{Eq. } \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_to_geometric_factors}}   \begin{array}{c}
      \text{Geometric} \\ \text{Factors}
    \end{array}
  \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

The above figure summarizes both the continuous and discrete representations of the Jacobian matrix. The discrete approach applies to any deformed hexahedral element. However, for a single hexahedral element, this method involves $3N_1^3$ memory references to node coordinates, 9 tensor contractions (totaling $18N_1^4$ FLOPs), and $N_1^3$ evaluations of Equation \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_to_geometric_factors}. Introducing this overhead into the axhelm kernel to replace $6N_1^3$ or $7N_1^3$ memory references for geometric factors is not worthwhile. However, when $\Phi^{(e)}$ has a simple analytical expression, $\mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}}$ often does as well. In this case, the continuous approach (Equation \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_at_node}) can efficiently compute the Jacobian matrices at each physical node.

The continuous (analytical) approach does not apply to all elements. Therefore, it must be designed for a specific type of hexahedral element: 1) It is sufficiently simple to ensure an analytical expression for its Jacobian matrix. 2) It is flexible enough to be widely applicable across various meshes. In Section \ref{trilinear_element_subsection}, we see that the trilinear element is a suitable candidate for this purpose.

\subsection{Low-Cost Recalculation of Geometric Factors for Trilinear Elements}
\label{trilinear_element_subsection}
\begin{definition}[Trilinear Element]
  \label{trilinear_element_definition}
   Given eight vertices coordinates $\mathbf{v}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$, $0 \leq i \leq 7$, a trilinear element is uniquely determined by the mapping $(x, y, z) = \Phi(r, s, t) := \sum_{i = 0}^{7} \sigma_i(r, s, t) \mathbf{v}_i,$
  where $(r, s, t) \in \hat{\Omega} = [-1, 1]^{3}$ and the functions $\sigma_i$ are defined as \\
  $
    \sigma_0(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 - r) (1 - s) (1 - t),
    \sigma_1(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 + r) (1 - s) (1 - t), \\
    \sigma_2(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 - r) (1 + s) (1 - t),
    \sigma_3(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 + r) (1 + s) (1 - t), \\
    \sigma_4(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 - r) (1 - s) (1 + t),
    \sigma_5(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 + r) (1 - s) (1 + t), \\
    \sigma_6(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 - r) (1 + s) (1 + t),
    \sigma_7(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} (1 + r) (1 + s) (1 + t).
  $
\end{definition}
As a fundamental Q1 type (with linear shape functions) in both geometric modeling and FEM, the trilinear element has long been favored by researcher \cite{A_performance_study_of_tetrahedral_and_hexahedral_elements, Trilinear_hexahedral_finite_elements_with_higher_order, An_efficient_3D_enhanced_strain_element, Higher_order_hierarchical_curved_hexahedral_vector, Improved_versions_of_assumed_enhanced_strain_tri_linear_elements, Poly_Spline_FEM}. As shown in Figure \ref{trilinear_element_fig} and Definition \ref{trilinear_element_definition}, a trilinear element is uniquely determined by eight freely chosen vertices\cite{High_Order_Methods_Incompressible_Fluid_Flow}. The flexibility allows for the arbitrary selection of vertices, which in turn determines the edges, faces, and the whole element. This enables the trilinear element to adapt to complex geometries and effectively fill the global domain $\Omega$. It is a feasible and common practice to fill $\Omega$ entirely using trilinear elements. If higher precision is needed, more sophisticated higher-order elements, such as Q2 or Q3 \cite{Poly_Spline_FEM}, are typically employed only at the boundaries of $\Omega$. Based on our statistics of the mesh (Figure \ref{fuel_rod_fig}) used in a typical NekRS application \cite{NekRS_first_paper, VisIt_website}, boundary elements account for only 11\%. This indicates that trilinear elements possess sufficient versatility and a high proportion of the overall mesh. Moreover, the trilinear element has an analytical Jacobian matrix.
\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figures/trilinear_element.pdf}
  \caption{A trilinear element: 1) Each edge is a line segment. 2) The line segment $\overline{\mathbf{v}_1\mathbf{v}_5}$ slides to $\overline{\mathbf{v}_0\mathbf{v}_4}$, forming a ruled surface, with similar surfaces for the other faces. By placing vertices around it, more trilinear elements can be expanded.}
  \label{trilinear_element_fig}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fuel-rod.pdf}
  \caption{A mesh: nuclear fuel rod cooling fluid simulation.}
  \label{fuel_rod_fig}
\end{figure}

Let $\mathbf{V}^{(e)} = (\mathbf{v}_0^\mathrm{T}, ..., \mathbf{v}_7^\mathrm{T})^\mathrm{T}$ is a matrix of size $8 \times 3$. It is also noted that the trilinear mapping has a compact tensor-product form:
\begin{equation}
  \label{trilinear_mapping_compact_form}
  \Phi^{(e)}(r, s, t) = \frac{1}{8} [(1-t, 1+t) \otimes (1-s, 1+s) \otimes (1-r, 1+r)] \mathbf{V}^{(e)}.
\end{equation}
Substituting Equation \eqref{trilinear_mapping_compact_form} into Equation \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_function}, we obtain
{\small
\begin{equation}
  \label{trilinear_Jacobian}
  \mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}}(r, s, t) =
  \frac{1}{8} {\mathbf{V}^{(e)}}^\mathrm{T}
  \begin{bmatrix}
    (1-t, 1+t) \otimes (1-s, 1+s) \otimes (-1, 1) \\ (1-t, 1+t) \otimes (-1, 1) \otimes (1-r, 1+r) \\ (-1, 1) \otimes (1-s, 1+s) \otimes (1-r, 1+r)
  \end{bmatrix}^\mathrm{T}.
\end{equation}
}
However, directly substituting $\mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}}(\xi_i, \xi_j, \xi_k)$ ($0 \leq i, j, k \leq N$) into Equation \eqref{trilinear_Jacobian} and expanding it to compute $\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}$ remains impractical. Because even a single node requires a $3 \times 3 \times 8$ matrix multiplication, the computational cost (144 FLOPs) remains high. To enhance efficiency, we must analyze the formula's structure, identify common terms, and extract invariant components for optimization. Based on this, we propose Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear}, with key design aspects as follows:

\begin{algorithm}
  \fontsize{7.8}{8.8}\selectfont
  \caption{Geometric factors Recalculation (trilinear)}
  \label{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear}
  \SetKwInOut{Input}{\textbf{Input}}
  \SetKwInOut{Output}{\textbf{Output}}
  \SetKwInOut{Init}{\textbf{Init}}
  \SetKw{KwDStep}{\textbf{step}}
  \SetKw{KwDAnd}{\textbf{and}}
  \SetKw{KwDIn}{\textbf{in}}
  \Input{$\mathbf{V}^{(e)}$: stored in s\_V[8][3].
  }
  e = blockIdx.x; j = threadIdx.y; i = threadIdx.x\;
  s = c\_gllx[j]; r = c\_gllx[i]; tmp[3] = \{0, 0, 0\}\;
  s0 = 1 - s; s1 = 1 + s; r0 = 1 - r; r1 = 1 + r\;
  \If{$j < 3$}{
    tmp1 = r0 * (s\_V[1][j] - s\_V[0][j]) + r1 * (s\_V[3][j] - s\_V[2][j])\;
    tmp2 = r0 * (s\_V[5][j] - s\_V[4][j]) + r1 * (s\_V[7][j] - s\_V[6][j])\;
    tmp3 = r0 * (s\_V[2][j] - s\_V[0][j]) + r1 * (s\_V[3][j] - s\_V[1][j])\;
    tmp4 = r0 * (s\_V[6][j] - s\_V[4][j]) + r1 * (s\_V[7][j] - s\_V[5][j])\;
    s\_E0[i][j] = tmp1 + tmp2; s\_E1[i][j] = tmp2 - tmp1\;
    s\_F0[i][j] = tmp3 + tmp4; s\_F1[i][j] = tmp4 - tmp3\;
  }
  \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{d} < 3$}{
    tmp[d]=r0*s0*(s\_V[4][d]-s\_V[0][d])+r1*s0*(s\_V[5][d]-s\_V[1][d])\quad+r1*s1*(s\_V[7][d]-s\_V[3][d])+r0*s1*(s\_V[6][d]-s\_V[2][d])\;
  }
  J02 = tmp[0]; J12 = tmp[1]; J22 = tmp[2]\;
  % J02 = r0 * s0 * (V[4][0] - V[0][0]) + r1 * s0 * (V[5][0] - V[1][0]) + r1 * s1 * (V[7][0] - V[3][0]) + r0 * s1 * (V[6][0] - V[2][0])\;
  % J12 = r0 * s0 * (V[4][1] - V[0][1]) + r1 * s0 * (V[5][1] - V[1][1]) + r1 * s1 * (V[7][1] - V[3][1]) + r0 * s1 * (V[6][1] - V[2][1])\;
  % J22 = r0 * s0 * (V[4][2] - V[0][2]) + r1 * s0 * (V[5][2] - V[1][2]) + r1 * s1 * (V[7][2] - V[3][2]) + r0 * s1 * (V[6][2] - V[2][2])\;
  gllw\_ij\_div\_8 = 0.125 * c\_gllw[i] * c\_gllw[j]\tcp*[r]{\color{gray} constant $\mathbf{W}_{N}$}
  syncthreads()\;
  \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{k} \leq N$}{
    t = c\_gllx[k]\tcp*[r]{\color{gray} 'c\_': constant memory copy of $\mathbf{\Xi}_{N}$}
    J00=s\_E0[j][0]+t*s\_E1[j][0];\quad J01=s\_F0[i][0]+t*s\_F1[i][0]\;
    J10=s\_E0[j][1]+t*s\_E1[j][1];\quad J11=s\_F0[i][1]+t*s\_F1[i][1]\;
    J20=s\_E0[j][2]+t*s\_E1[j][2];\quad J21=s\_F0[i][2]+t*s\_F1[i][2]\;    

    JJ00=J00*J00+J10*J10+J20*J20;\quad JJ01=J00*J01+J10*J11+J20*J21\;
    JJ02=J00*J02+J10*J12+J20*J22;\quad JJ11=J01*J01+J11*J11+J21*J21\;
    JJ12=J01*J02+J11*J12+J21*J22;\quad JJ22=J02*J02+J12*J12+J22*J22\;

    detJ = J00 * (J11 * J22 - J21 * J12) - J10 * (J01 * J22 - J21 * J02)\qquad\qquad + J20 * (J01 * J12 - J11 * J02)\;
    gScale = gllw\_ij\_div\_8 * c\_gllw[k] / detJ\;
    g00=(JJ11*JJ22-JJ12*JJ12)*gScale; g01=(JJ02*JJ12-JJ01*JJ22)*gScale\;
    g02=(JJ01*JJ12-JJ02*JJ11)*gScale; g11=(JJ00*JJ22-JJ02*JJ02)*gScale\;
    g12=(JJ01*JJ02-JJ00*JJ12)*gScale; g22=(JJ00*JJ11-JJ01*JJ01)*gScale\;
    \If{$\mathrm{isHelm}$}{
      gwj = gScale * detJ * detJ * 0.015625\;
    }
  }
\end{algorithm}

1) \textbf{Invariant Components} (Lines 11-13): Each thread $(i, j)$ needs to compute $N_1$ matrices of $\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}$ ($0 \leq k \leq N$). It is important to note that the third column of $\mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}}(r, s, t)$ in Equation \eqref{trilinear_Jacobian} depends only on $r$ and $s$, meaning the third column of $\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}$ is only dependent on $i$ and $j$. Therefore, this column only needs to be computed once and can be shared across the $k$-loop.

2) \textbf{Common Terms} (Lines 4-10): Noting that the transpose of the first column of $\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}$ is given by (the second column is analogous)
\begin{equation}
  \label{trilinear_common_terms}
  \begin{matrix}
     & [(1-\xi_k, 1+\xi_k) \otimes (1-\xi_j, 1+\xi_j) \otimes (-1, 1)] {\mathbf{V}^{(e)}} =       \\
     & \quad [(1, 1) \otimes (1-\xi_j, 1+\xi_j) \otimes (-1, 1)] {\mathbf{V}^{(e)}} + \mathrel{\phantom{-\xi_k}}          \\
     & \quad [(-1, 1) \otimes (1-\xi_j, 1+\xi_j) \otimes (-1, 1)] {\mathbf{V}^{(e)}} \cdot \xi_k.
  \end{matrix}
\end{equation}
Here, Except for $\xi_k$, the remaining parts are shared among threads $(i, j)$ with the same $j$. Therefore, we can precompute four matrices, $\mathbf{E}_0$, $\mathbf{E}_1$, $\mathbf{F}_0$, and $\mathbf{F}_1$, each of size $N_1 \times 3$, before the $k$-loop and store them in shared memory to eliminate redundant computations:
\begin{equation}
  \begin{matrix}
    &\mathbf{E}_0 = \mathrel{\phantom{-}}[(1, 1) \otimes (\mathbf{1}-\mathbf{\Xi}_{N}, \mathbf{1}+\mathbf{\Xi}_{N}) \otimes (-1, 1)] \mathbf{V}^{(e)}, \\
    &\mathbf{E}_1 = [(-1, 1) \otimes (\mathbf{1}-\mathbf{\Xi}_{N}, \mathbf{1}+\mathbf{\Xi}_{N}) \otimes (-1, 1)] \mathbf{V}^{(e)}, \\
    &\mathbf{F}_0 = \mathrel{\phantom{-}}[(1, 1) \otimes (-1, 1) \otimes (\mathbf{1}-\mathbf{\Xi}_{N}, \mathbf{1}+\mathbf{\Xi}_{N})] \mathbf{V}^{(e)}, \\
    &\mathbf{F}_1 = [(-1, 1) \otimes (-1, 1) \otimes (\mathbf{1}-\mathbf{\Xi}_{N}, \mathbf{1}+\mathbf{\Xi}_{N})] \mathbf{V}^{(e)}, \\
  \end{matrix}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{1}$ is an $N_1$-dimensional column vector of ones. Thanks to the common terms, the six values in the first and second columns of $\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}$ can be recomputed with only 12 FLOPs (Lines 18-20).

3) \textbf{Jacobian Matrix to Geometric Factors} (Lines 21-30): After obtaining the Jacobian matrix $\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}$ of a node, for the computation in Equation \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_to_geometric_factors}, we first compute $\mathbf{K} = {\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}}^\mathrm{T} \mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}$ and then 
\begin{equation}
  \label{geometric_factors_from_Jacobian_in_tri_algo}
  |\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}| \cdot {\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}}^{-1} {\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}}^\mathrm{-T} = |\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}| \cdot \mathbf{K}^{-1} = |\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}| \cdot \frac{\text{adj}(\mathbf{K})}{|\mathbf{K}|} = \frac{\text{adj}(\mathbf{K})}{|\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)}|},
\end{equation}
where $\text{adj}(\mathbf{K})$ is the adjugate matrix of $\mathbf{K}$. Meanwhile, introduced by $\frac{1}{8}$ and the GLL weights, the scaling coefficient (gScale) is deferred to the final stage of the algorithm. In summary, Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear} replaces the memory references for the geometric factors with 24 memory references for $\mathbf{V}^{(e)}$ and $72N_1 + 51N_1^2 + (82 + \mathrm{isHelm} \times 3)N_1^3$ FLOPs.

For the special case of trilinear elements, parallelepipeds, Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_parallelepiped} enables geometric factor recomputation at virtually zero cost, since $\mathbf{J}^{\Phi^{(e)}}(r, s, t) \equiv \mathbf{J}^{(e)}$ remains constant. further, at each node $(e, i, j, k)$, $\mathbf{J}_{ijk}^{(e)} = \mathbf{J}^{(e)}$. Thus, in Equation \eqref{Jacobian_matrix_to_geometric_factors}, the geometric factors excluding GLL weights, i.e., $|\mathbf{J}^{(e)}| \cdot {\mathbf{J}^{(e)}}^{-1} {\mathbf{J}^{(e)}}^\mathrm{-T}$ and $|\mathbf{J}^{(e)}|$, can be get with just seven memory references. This property effectively removes the bottleneck associated with the geometric factors, making performance entirely dependent on tensor contractions.

\begin{algorithm}
  \fontsize{7.8}{7.8}\selectfont
  \caption{\small Geometric factors Recalculation (parallelepiped)}
  \label{algorithm_geometric_factors_parallelepiped}
  \SetKwInOut{Input}{\textbf{Input}}
  \SetKwInOut{Output}{\textbf{Output}}
  \SetKwInOut{Init}{\textbf{Init}}
  \SetKw{KwDStep}{\textbf{step}}
  \SetKw{KwDAnd}{\textbf{and}}
  \SetKw{KwDIn}{\textbf{in}}
  \Input{$h_{00}^{(e)}, ..., h_{22}^{(e)}$, and $h_\mathrm{det}^{(e)}$: geometric factors without GLL weights, stored in shared memory s\_h[7].
  }
  e = blockIdx.x; j = threadIdx.y; i = threadIdx.x\;
  h00 = s\_h[0]; h01 = s\_h[1]; h02 = s\_h[2]\;
  h11 = s\_h[3]; h12 = s\_h[4]; h22 = s\_h[5]; hdet= s\_h[6]\;
  gllw\_ij = c\_gllw[i] * c\_gllw[j]\;
  \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{k} \leq N$}{
    w = gllw\_ij * c\_gllw[k]; gwj = w * hdet\;
    g00 = w * h00; g01 = w * h01; g02 = w * h02\;
    g11 = w * h11; g12 = w * h12; g22 = w * h22\;
  }
\end{algorithm}





\section{Implementation and optimization}
\label{Implementation_and_optimization_section}

\subsection{Optimization of Geometric Factors}
\label{Optimization_of_Geometric_Factors}
The optimization in this Section is discussed in terms of two types of equations, focusing on the trilinear element.

\subsubsection{Merging Scalar Factors}
\label{Merging_Scalar_Factors_subsec}
For the Helmholtz equation, the axhelm kernel requires each node to access the scalar factors $\lambda_0$ and $\lambda_1$. It is important to note that: 1) In Algorithm \ref{algorithm_axhelm_original}, both $\lambda_0$ and $\lambda_1$ must be multiplied by the geometric factor. 2) In Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear}, the recalculation of the geometric factor also involves a scaling coefficient, gScale (considered as a custom geometric factor). We can multiply gScale and gwj with $\lambda_0$ and $\lambda_1$, respectively:
\begin{equation*}
  \mathbf{\Lambda}_2^{(e)} = \mathbf{G}_\mathrm{scale}^{(e)} \mathbf{\Lambda}_0^{(e)}, \qquad \text{and} \qquad \mathbf{\Lambda}_3^{(e)} = \mathbf{G}_\mathrm{wj}^{(e)} \mathbf{\Lambda}_1^{(e)}.
\end{equation*}
The computation of $\mathbf{\Lambda}_2^{(e)}$ and $\mathbf{\Lambda}_3^{(e)}$ will be completed before starting to solve the linear system, and they will be used as new scalar factors to be read by the axhelm kernel. This eliminates the computations related to gScale and gwj (Lines 24, 25, and 30) in Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear}. Moreover, the costly division operations \cite{Floating_Point_Division_on_GPU} are no longer needed.

\subsubsection{Partial Recalculation}
\label{Partial_Recalculation_subsec}
For the Poisson equation, the optimization in Section \ref{Merging_Scalar_Factors_subsec} is no longer effective because the axhelm in this case no longer involves $\mathbf{\Lambda}_0^{(e)}$, $\mathbf{\Lambda}_1^{(e)}$, and $\mathbf{G}_\mathrm{wj}^{(e)}$. However, we can revert the scaling coefficient gScale in Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear} to be obtained through memory access, instead of being recomputed in kernels (Lines 24-25). We refer to this approach as partial recomputation. This approach substitutes complex computation, including division, with memory accesses, making it a balanced solution.

Table \ref{table_overhead_recomputing_geometric_factors} summarizes the overhead of various geometric factor acquisition approaches discussed in this paper and other existing methods, highlighting the flexibility in this process. Notably, our proposed approach exhibits significantly lower overhead compared to the others. In theory, numerous viable approaches exist, each corresponding to a distinct roofline model.
