\subsection{Optimization of Tensor Contractions}
\label{tensor_contraction_optimization_subsection}
\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{figures/tensor_structure.pdf}
  \caption{A third-order tensor, its slices, and fibers.}
  \label{fig_tensor_structure}
\end{figure}
The majority of the computations in axhelm result from tensor contractions (Section \ref{Performance_bottleneck_analysis_of_axhelm_subsection}). With the alleviation of the memory access bottleneck for geometric factors, tensor contraction now becomes the most time-critical operation to be accelerated.

As shown in Equation \eqref{sum_factorization_eq}, a vector of size $N_1^3$ can be represented by a 3D index $(i, j, k)$, where $0 \leq i, j, k \leq N$. This vector can be treated as a third-order tensor \cite{Tensor_Decompositions_and_Applications}. As shown in Figure \ref{fig_tensor_structure}, a slice of a third-order tensor is a matrix obtained by fixing one of the indices, while a fiber is a vector obtained by fixing all but one of the indices \cite{Tensor_Decompositions_and_Applications}. In Algorithm \ref{algorithm_axhelm_original}, each thread $(i, j)$ owns a fiber $\mathbf{x}(i, j, :)$.
For a fixed $k$, the tensor contractions of $\mathbf{D}_r$, $\mathbf{D}_s$ and $\mathbf{D}_t$ in Equation \eqref{sum_factorization_eq}, corresponding to Lines 14-16, can be interpreted as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \setlength{\itemsep}{0pt}
  \setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
  \setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
  \item Matrix multiplication: $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$ and the $k$-th slice $\mathbf{x}(:, :, k)$.
  \item Matrix multiplication: $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$ and the transpose of $\mathbf{x}(:, :, k)$.
  \item Inner product: the $k$-th row of $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$ and each fiber $\mathbf{x}(i, j, :)$.
\end{itemize}

In summary, tensor contraction consists of many small BLAS operations per element, making its optimization most effective when following small BLAS tuning techniques. When global memory accesses can no longer be further reduced, the optimization effort should focus on reducing shared memory access overhead. However, in the original axhelm kernels, each thread needs to access shared memory $10N_1^2$ times to complete the tensor contraction (Algorithm \ref{algorithm_axhelm_original}: Lines 14-16 and 23-25). When focusing solely on tensor contraction, the ratio of shared memory accesses to FLOPs is as high as 10:12. To address this, we present an optimized approach in Algorithm \ref{algorithm_tensor_cores_acceleration_tensor_contraction}, where the tensor contractions of $\mathbf{D}_r$, $\mathbf{D}_s$ and $\mathbf{D}_t$ are optimized separately ($\mathbf{D}_r^\mathrm{T}$, $\mathbf{D}_s^\mathrm{T}$, and $\mathbf{D}_t^\mathrm{T}$ are analogous).

% In summary, the computational pattern of tensor contraction in axhelm inherently requires each element to perform a large number of small BLAS operations, whose performance is typically much lower than that of a single large BLAS operation \cite{The_Design_and_Performance_of_Batched_BLAS}.
% Therefore, we cannot rely on standard libraries like cuBLAS \cite{cuBLAS_Library_User_Guide} and rocBLAS \cite{rocBLAS_Library_User_Guide}, as they do not provide high performance \cite{Optimization_Ax_Nekbone_SC_20_Poster}.
% Consequently, the optimization strategy for tensor contractions should follow the optimization methods of small BLAS operators.
% The shared memory strategy is often a key focus in optimizing the GEMM (General Matrix Multiplication) operator on GPGPUs \cite{Performance_Design_Autotuning_Batched_GEMM_for_GPUs,very_small_GEMM}.
% Previous studies have shown that tensor contraction operations in the SEM and high-order FEM are constrained by shared memory bandwidth \cite{Accelerate_tensor_product_operations_with_tensor_cores}.
% Therefore, optimization efforts should focus on reducing shared memory accesses to enhance computational throughput \cite{Acceleration_tensor_product_operations_in_FEM}.

\begin{algorithm}
  \fontsize{7.8}{7.8}\selectfont
  \caption{Accelerating tensor contractions}
  \label{algorithm_tensor_cores_acceleration_tensor_contraction}
  \SetKwInOut{Input}{\textbf{Input}}
  \SetKwInOut{Output}{\textbf{Output}}
  \SetKwInOut{Init}{\textbf{Init}}
  \SetKw{KwDStep}{\textbf{step}}
  \SetKw{KwDAnd}{\textbf{and}}
  \SetKw{KwDIn}{\textbf{in}}
  \Input{$\mathbf{X}^{(e)}$.}
  e = blockIdx.x; j = threadIdx.y; i = threadIdx.x\;
  warp = (j * blockDim.x + i) / warpSize\;
  s\_Dhat[j][i] = c\_Dhat[j][i]\tcp*[r]{\color{gray} get $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$(j,i) from constant copy}
  syncthreads()\;
  \textbf{LOAD}:\ \ fragD\_left,\ \ fragD\_right,\ \ fragDT\_upper,\ \ fragDT\_lower\tcp*[r]{\color{gray}each fragment from one half of s\_Dhat or $\text{s\_Dhat}^\text{T}$}
  \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{k} \leq N$}{
    x[k] = $\mathbf{X}^{(e)}$(i,j,k)\;
  }
  syncthreads()\;
  \For{$k = 0$ \KwTo $N$ \KwDStep 2}{
    s\_x[0][j][i] = x[k];\quad s\_x[1][j][i] = x[k + 1]\;
    xr[2] = \{0, 0\};\quad xs[2] = \{0, 0\};\quad xt[2] = \{0, 0\}\;
    \For{$0 \leq \mathrm{m} \leq N$}{
      xt[0] += c\_Dhat[k][m] * x[m]\tcp*[r]{\color{gray} use constant copy}
      xt[1] += c\_Dhat[k + 1][m] * x[m]\;
    }
    syncthreads()\;
    % fill\_fragment(fragAcc, 0.0f)\;
    \textbf{FILL}: fragAcc = 0\;
    % load\_mat\_sync(fragB, \&s\_q[warp][0][0], 8)\;
    \textbf{LOAD}: fragB = upper half of s\_x[warp]\;
    % mma\_sync(fragAcc, D\_\_left, fragB, fragAcc)\;
    \textbf{MMA}: fragAcc += fragD\_left * fragB\;
    % load\_mat\_sync(fragB, \&s\_q[warp][4][0], 8)\;
    \textbf{LOAD}: fragB = lower half of s\_x[warp]\;
    % mma\_sync(fragAcc, D\_\_right, fragB, fragAcc)\;
    \textbf{MMA}: fragAcc += fragD\_right * fragB\;
    % store\_mat\_sync(\&s\_Gqs[warp][0][0], fragAcc)\;
    \textbf{STORE}: s\_gxs[warp] = fragAcc\;

    % fill\_fragment(fragAcc, 0.0f)\;
    \textbf{FILL}: fragAcc = 0\;
    % load\_mat\_sync(fragA, \&s\_q[warp][0][0], 8)\;
    \textbf{LOAD}: fragA = left half of s\_x[warp]\;
    % mma\_sync(fragAcc, fragA, DT\_upper, fragAcc)\;
    \textbf{MMA}: fragAcc += fragA * fragDT\_upper\;
    % load\_mat\_sync(fragA, \&s\_q[warp][0][4], 8)\;
    \textbf{LOAD}: fragA = right half of s\_x[warp]\;
    % mma\_sync(fragAcc, fragA, DT\_lower, fragAcc)\;
    \textbf{MMA}: fragAcc += fragA * fragDT\_lower\;
    % store\_mat\_sync(\&s\_Gqr[warp][0][0], fragAcc)\;
    \textbf{STORE}: s\_gxr[warp] = fragAcc\;
    syncthreads()\;

    xs[0] = s\_gxs[0][j][i];\quad xr[0] = s\_gxr[0][j][i]\;
    xs[1] = s\_gxs[1][j][i];\quad xr[1] = s\_gxr[1][j][i]\;
  }
\end{algorithm}

\subsubsection{Computing $\mathbf{D}_r$ and $\mathbf{D}_s$ on Tensor Cores}

% Since NVIDIA introduced Tensor Cores in its Volta GPU architecture in 2017 \cite{NVIDIA_Tensor_Core_Programmability}, subsequent architectures such as Turing and Ampere have continued to incorporate this technology. Designed specifically to accelerate matrix multiplication operations, Tensor Cores (on some platforms referred to as Matrix Cores or Cube Units) have become a highly sought-after feature. Today, this technology is widely adopted across various platforms, including AMD GPUs, Hygon DCU K100-AI, and Huawei Ascend AI accelerators \cite{On_the_Rise_of_AMD_Matrix_Cores, Ascend_a_Scalable_and_Unified_Architecture}. In HPC applications, matrix multiplication acceleration can significantly benefit from Tensor Cores. It is worth noting that this performance advantage remains considerable even in the case of batched small matrix multiplications \cite{NVIDIA_Tensor_Core_Programmability}.

% Lower-level compiler abstraction layers, such as Compiler Intrinsics and PTX, provide more direct and flexible access, allowing fine-grained control over all the functionalities of the Tensor cores \cite{On_the_Rise_of_AMD_Matrix_Cores}. 

The tensor contractions of $\mathbf{D}_r$ and $\mathbf{D}_s$ are equivalent to matrix multiplication. Therefore, if the platform supports dedicated matrix multiplication acceleration units—such as Tensor Cores \cite{NVIDIA_Tensor_Core_Programmability}, Matrix Cores \cite{On_the_Rise_of_AMD_Matrix_Cores}, or Cube Units \cite{Ascend_a_Scalable_and_Unified_Architecture}—offloading these computations to these units is an effective way to improve computational efficiency. In this paper, we use the term "Tensor Core" to refer to such units. The Warp/Wave Matrix Multiply-Accumulate (WMMA) API is a widely adopted approach for leveraging Tensor Cores, which streamlines matrix multiplication programming by offering abstractions at the warp or wavefront level \cite{NVIDIA_Tensor_Core_Programmability, On_the_Rise_of_AMD_Matrix_Cores}. The WMMA API relies on the concept of fragments, which map matrix elements to the registers of individual threads, and provides the following matrix operations: 1) \textbf{LOAD}: load a matrix block from shared/global memory to a fragment. 2) \textbf{MMA}: perform matrix multiplication and accumulate the result. 3) \textbf{STORE}: store the result back to shared/global memory. These operations require all threads within a warp/wavefront to work cooperatively. Tensor cores typically support a variety of data types and MMA sizes. For example, on NVIDIA platforms, $16 \times 16 \times 16$ for FP16, $8 \times 8 \times 4$ for FP64, and so on.

As long as the order $N$ is properly chosen, such as in the case shown in Algorithm \ref{algorithm_tensor_cores_acceleration_tensor_contraction} ($N + 1 = 8$, warp size = 32, and MMA size = $8 \times 8 \times 4$), the computational benefits provided by Tensor Cores can be fully utilized. Since an $8 \times 8$ thread block contains two warps, we unfold $k$-loop with a step size of 2 (Line 9). In each iteration, the two warps in the thread block independently compute a layer of tensor contractions of $\mathbf{D}_r$ and $\mathbf{D}_s$ (lines 16-27). Notably, Algorithm \ref{algorithm_tensor_cores_acceleration_tensor_contraction} transposes the tensor contraction of $\mathbf{D}_r$, ensuring that $\text{s\_gxr}$ is accessed with $j$ as the row index and $i$ as the column index. This arrangement helps reduce bank conflicts.

\subsubsection{Optimization Related to $\mathbf{D}_t$}
Since the GLL points, weights, and differentiation matrix $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$ are constants dependent on $N$, we can pre-prepare their constant memory copies. For tensor contraction of $\mathbf{D}_t$ (Lines 12-14), since it is essentially a vector inner product operation, we still rely on general-purpose computing units (e.g., CUDA Cores and Stream Processors) on GPGPUs for execution. However, in this computation, each thread $(i, j)$ accesses $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$ in an identical pattern, meaning that the access depends only on $k, m$ and is independent of $i, j$. Given this characteristic, we choose to read from the constant memory copy of $\hat{\mathbf{D}}_N$, denoted as $\text{c\_Dhat}$, instead of using shared memory $\text{s\_Dhat}$. This choice provides two advantages: 1) Constant memory is equipped with a dedicated cache, enabling all threads to efficiently read the same data via broadcast, reducing memory access latency. 2) Unlike shared memory, bank conflicts do not affect constant memory.

In Algorithm \ref{algorithm_tensor_cores_acceleration_tensor_contraction} (Lines 12-30), to compute the tensor contractions of $\mathbf{D}_r$, $\mathbf{D}_s$ and $\mathbf{D}_t$ and store the results back into registers, each thread needs to access shared memory a total of $6N_1$ times. This is notably fewer than the $5N_1^2$ accesses in Algorithm \ref{algorithm_axhelm_original} (Lines 14-16).

\subsection{Roofline Model of Axhelm}
\label{Roofline_model_for_axhelm_subsection}
When evaluating the performance of the optimized axhelm kernels, it is essential to exclude the recalculation cost from the effective computational workload. The two performance formulas are:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{P}_{\text{eff}} = \frac{\mathcal{F}_\text{ax}}{\mathcal{T}_\text{real}} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{tot}} = \frac{\mathcal{F}_\text{tot}}{\mathcal{T}_\text{real}} = \frac{\mathcal{F}_\text{ax} + \mathcal{F}_{\text{reGeo}}}{\mathcal{T}_\text{real}},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{T}_\text{real}$ denotes the actual execution time averaged per element in the axhelm kernel. $\mathcal{P}_{\text{eff}}$ represents the effective performance of the axhelm kernel, while $\mathcal{P}_{\text{tot}}$ indicates the total performance.

The theoretical lower bound of $\mathcal{T}_\text{real}$ is determined by the maximum of the global memory access time and the computation time, expressed as $\mathcal{T}_{\text{min}} = \max\left(\mathcal{T}_\text{mem}, \mathcal{T}_\text{cmp}\right)$, where
\begin{equation}
  \label{theoretical_minimum_time_eq}
    \mathcal{T}_\text{mem} = \frac{\mathcal{M}_\text{XYL} + \mathcal{M}_\text{geo}}{\mathcal{B}}, \mathcal{T}_\text{cmp} = \frac{\mathcal{F}_\text{rs}}{\mathcal{P}_{\text{peakTC}}} + \frac{\mathcal{F}_\text{ax} + \mathcal{F}_{\text{reGeo}} - \mathcal{F}_\text{rs}}{\mathcal{P}_{\text{peakGC}}}.
\end{equation}
Here: 1) $\mathcal{F}_\text{ax}$, $\mathcal{M}_\text{XYL}$, $\mathcal{F}_\text{reGeo}$, and $\mathcal{M}_\text{geo}$ are provided in Table \ref{table_workload_memory_access}, \ref{table_overhead_recomputing_geometric_factors}, and Equation \eqref{other_memory_access}. 2) $\mathcal{F}_\text{rs} = 8N_1^3 \times d$ is the computational workload of tensor contractions ($\mathbf{D}_r$, $\mathbf{D}_s$, $\mathbf{D}_r^\mathrm{T}$, $\mathbf{D}_s^\mathrm{T}$) that can be offloaded to Tensor Cores. 3) $\mathcal{P}_{\text{peakTC}}$ and $\mathcal{P}_{\text{peakGC}}$ represent the platform's peak when using Tensor Cores and general-purpose cores, respectively. If the platform does not have Tensor Cores, then $\mathcal{P}_{\text{peakTC}} = \mathcal{P}_{\text{peakGC}}$.

Similarly, the roofline models can be expressed as two types:
\begin{equation}
  \label{novel_roofline_model_eq}
  \mathcal{R}_{\text{eff}} = \frac{\mathcal{F}_{\text{ax}}}{\mathcal{T}_{\text{min}}} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathcal{R}_{\text{tot}} = \frac{\mathcal{F}_{\text{ax}} + \mathcal{F}_{\text{reGeo}}}{\mathcal{T}_{\text{min}}}.
\end{equation}
The two performance metrics and their corresponding roofline models have the same efficiency model $\mathcal{E} = \frac{\mathcal{P}_{\text{eff}}}{\mathcal{R}_{\text{eff}}} = \frac{\mathcal{P}_{\text{tot}}}{\mathcal{R}_{\text{tot}}}$.
Given that $\mathcal{P}_{\text{eff}}$ is more convenient for measurement and comparison, Section \ref{Experiments_section} predominantly uses $\mathcal{P}_{\text{eff}}$ and $\mathcal{R}_{\text{eff}}$.





