\subsection{Roofline Analysis of axhelm}
Based on the roofline model described in Section \ref{Roofline_model_for_axhelm_subsection}, along with the costs of various geometric factor retrieval schemes listed in Table \ref{table_overhead_recomputing_geometric_factors}, we analyze the roofline performance of the axhelm kernels on both target platforms, with results shown in Figures \ref{fig_roofline_A100} and \ref{fig_roofline_K100}. Apart from using Tensor Cores, other optimizations related to tensor contraction do not influence the roofline model.

Except for the case (A100, Poisson, $d$ = 1), the memory access time $\mathcal{T}_\text{mem}$ is always greater than the computation time $\mathcal{T}_\text{cmp}$, especially on the K100, where $\mathcal{T}_\text{mem}$ is significantly higher than $\mathcal{T}_\text{cmp}$. This indicates that the roofline model is almost always dominated by $\mathcal{T}_\text{mem}$. On both platforms, the recalculation of geometric factors (Algorithms \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear} and \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_parallelepiped}) significantly reduces $\mathcal{T}_\text{mem}$. The additional computation time introduced by Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_parallelepiped} can be ignored, while Algorithm \ref{algorithm_geometric_factors_trilinear} introduces a certain amount of computation overhead. In any case, they significantly improve the roofline performance and expand the optimization space.

On the A100, Tensor Cores significantly reduce $\mathcal{T}_\text{cmp}$. On both platforms, the merging scalar factors optimization (Section \ref{Merging_Scalar_Factors_subsec}) for the Helmholtz case is always effective as it solely reduces $\mathcal{T}_\text{cmp}$. However, for the Poisson case, the effectiveness of the partial recalculation (Section \ref{Partial_Recalculation_subsec}) depends on practical performance.


